      "id": "the-greeters-lament",
      "title": "The Greeter's Lament",
      "alternateTitle": "The Greeter's Lament",
      "act": "Act II: The Downstream Citizens",
      "actNumber": 2,
      "audioFile": "assets/audio/05-the-greeters-lament.mp3",
      "tagline": "For the unretired: standing at the superstore doors under fluorescent lights.",
      "theory": "Real Purchasing Power Destruction: the unretiring of elderly Americans whose pensions were liquidated by stealth inflation.",
      "tempo": "Slow, Aching Country Blues (72 BPM)",
      "leadArchetype": "The Unretiree (The Greeter)",
      "summary": "\"The Greeter's Lament\" is a slow, heartbreaking dirge for the end of the American Dream of retirement. It tells the story of \"Old Man Miller,\" who did everything right—saved his money and followed the rules—only to find that inflation turned his savings to ash . Now, at 75 , he stands at the entrance of a big-box store, greeting customers and \"trading minutes for a little bit of cash\" . Musically, it is sparse and lonely, featuring a Dobro (slide guitar) over the faint, ambient beep of checkout scanners .",
      "decoratedLyrics": "[Genre: Industrial Dark Americana, Slowcore Blues]\n[Mood: Heartbreaking, Lonely, Resigned, Ambient]\n[Vocalist: Male | Baritone | Aged/Raspy | Intimate]\n[Instrumentation: Acoustic Slide Guitar (Dobro), Ambient Superstore Noise, Faint Synth Drone, Slow Footstomp]\n[Tempo: Very Slow | Dirge-like | Free time]\n[Intro]\n[Soundscape: The ambient hum of a large store. Faint beeping of scanners. The sound of sliding glass doors opening and closing.]\n[Instrumental: A slow, mournful slide guitar melody enters. High reverb. Lonely.]\n[Verse 1]\n[Vocal Delivery: Spoken word | Weary]\nThe automatic door slides open wide\nA rush of air from the cool inside.\nI put on the vest, I put on the smile\nI’ve been standing on this concrete for a long, long while.\n\"Welcome in,\" I say to the face\nPassing through the gates of the marketplace.\nThey don't see me, they look right through\nJust a ghost in a vest of royal blue.\n[Chorus]\n[Instrumentation: A deep, slow bass note sustains]\n[Vocal Style: Sung | Melodic | Fragile]\nI saved the paper, but the paper turned to ash\nNow I'm trading minutes for a little bit of cash.\nI thought I bought my rest.\nI thought I passed the test.\nBut the dollar died in my chest\nYeah, the dollar died in my chest.\n[Interlude]\n[Soundscape: \"Price check on aisle four\" (muffled PA announcement)]\n[Instrumental: The Dobro slides up and down, mimicking a human moan.]\n[Verse 2]\n[Vocal Delivery: Quiet | Sad]\nThe shelf is full of things I used to know\nWhen the river was high and the debt was low.\nNow I check the receipt and I check the bag\nWhile my knees give out and my shoulders sag.\nSeventy-four years to end up here\nWatching the seasons disappear.\nThe pension is gone, the house is a lien\nFed to the mouth of the big machine.\n[Bridge]\n[Tempo: Slows down even more]\n[Vocal Emotion: Breaking]\nI skip the pill to buy the bread.\n[spoken] That’s the choice now.\nA roof above or a clear head.\nThe golden years are painted rust\nAnd the savings account is full of dust.\n[Guitar Solo]\n[Instrumental: Weeping slide guitar solo. Very emotional. Minimal percussion.]\n[Chorus]\n[Vocal Style: Sung with a harmony (ghostly)]\nI saved the paper, but the paper turned to ash\nNow I'm trading minutes for a little bit of cash.\nI thought I bought my rest.\nI thought I passed the test.\nBut the dollar died in my chest.\n[Outro]\n[Soundscape: The beeping gets louder, then fades.]\n[Vocal: Spoken | Whisper]\nWelcome to the store.\nHave a nice day.\n[Sound: The sliding door closes shut]\n[End]",
      "cleanLyrics": "[Intro]\n[Verse 1]\nThe automatic door slides open wide\nA rush of air from the cool inside.\nI put on the vest, I put on the smile\nI’ve been standing on this concrete for a long, long while.\n\"Welcome in,\" I say to the face\nPassing through the gates of the marketplace.\nThey don't see me, they look right through\nJust a ghost in a vest of royal blue.\n[Chorus]\nI saved the paper, but the paper turned to ash\nNow I'm trading minutes for a little bit of cash.\nI thought I bought my rest.\nI thought I passed the test.\nBut the dollar died in my chest\nYeah, the dollar died in my chest.\n[Interlude]\n[Verse 2]\nThe shelf is full of things I used to know\nWhen the river was high and the debt was low.\nNow I check the receipt and I check the bag\nWhile my knees give out and my shoulders sag.\nSeventy-four years to end up here\nWatching the seasons disappear.\nThe pension is gone, the house is a lien\nFed to the mouth of the big machine.\n[Bridge]\nI skip the pill to buy the bread.\nThat’s the choice now.\nA roof above or a clear head.\nThe golden years are painted rust\nAnd the savings account is full of dust.\n[Guitar Solo]\n[Chorus]\nI saved the paper, but the paper turned to ash\nNow I'm trading minutes for a little bit of cash.\nI thought I bought my rest.\nI thought I passed the test.\nBut the dollar died in my chest.\n[Outro]\nWelcome to the store.\nHave a nice day.\n(Door Closes)\n© 2025 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
      "musicalPrompt": "[Genre: Industrial Dark Americana]\n[Style: Slowcore Blues]\n[Mood: Heartbreaking, Lonely, Resigned, Ambient]\n[Vocalist: Male | Baritone | Aged/Raspy | Intimate | \"Old Man\"]\n[Instrumentation: Acoustic Slide Guitar (Dobro), Minimal Footstomp, Ambient Superstore Sounds, Faint Drone]\n[Production: Spacious, Reverb on Guitar, Dry Vocals]\n[Structure: Slow Dirge, Free Time feel]",
