      "leadArchetype": "The Crowded-Out Entrepreneur",
      "summary": "\"Crowded Out (Main Street Blues)\" is a gritty, foot-stomping blues track that articulates the anger of the small business owner. It tells the story of the \"Smith Brothers,\" who survived wars and recessions but cannot survive the government sucking all the capital out of the room (The Crowding Out Effect)5. With the Treasury borrowing trillions, there is no money left for the \"plow\"6. The song uses the sound of a foreclosure padlock snapping and a metal shop shutter slamming as the primary rhythmic drivers.",
      "decoratedLyrics": "[Genre: Industrial Blues Rock]\n[Mood: Angry, Gritty, Blue-Collar, Heavy]\n[Vocalist: Male | Baritone | Raspy | Aggressive | \"Working Man\"]\n[Instrumentation: Distorted Harmonica, Heavy Boot Stomp, Metal Shutter Snare, Slide Guitar, Chain Rattles]\n[Tempo: Medium | Stomp Rhythm | Swing feel]\n[Intro]\n[Soundscape: The loud, echoing slam of a metal security shutter rolling down. SLAM. SLAM.]\n[Instrumental: A dirty, distorted harmonica riff plays over a heavy boot-stomp beat.]\n[Verse 1]\n[Vocal Delivery: Gritty | Resentful]\nThe Smith boys ran the shop for forty years\nThrough the blood and the sweat and the rust and the tears.\nThey weathered the storm and they weathered the drought\nBut the Big Man came and he crowded them out.\nI went to the bank with my hat in my hand\nNeed a little line just to work the land.\nThe banker said \"Son, I can't help you now\nThe Government bought the horse and the cow.\"\n[Chorus]\n[Rhythm: Heavy Stomp | Industrial Clank]\n[Vocal Style: Shouted | Bluesy]\nCrowded out!\nGot no room to breathe.\nCrowded out!\nGot no choice but to leave.\nThe Giant eats the grain, the mouse gets the husk\nThey closed the factory down at dusk.\nYeah, the Treasury takes the silver and gold\nAnd leaves us out in the bitter cold.\nCrowded out!\n[Interlude]\n[Instrumental: The sound of a foreclosure padlock snapping shut. CLICK-CLACK. Followed by a distorted slide guitar solo.]\n[Verse 2]\n[Vocal Delivery: Spoken word | Cynical]\nOne Point Eight Trill-yun in the hole this year\n[spoken] That’s the deficit.\nSucking up the cash, making money dear.\nThe Credit Crunch is a heavy boot\nStomping on the neck of the local root.\nSub-chap-ter Five filings on the rise\n[spoken] That's bankruptcy.\nWhile the stock market climbs to the empty skies.\nThey pave the road to a bridge nowhere\nWhile the shop floor sits in the silent air.\n[Bridge]\n[Tempo: Slows down | Heavy tension]\n[Instrumentation: Harmonica wails like a siren]\n[Vocal Style: Growling]\nThe interest rate is a nine percent wall.\nYou can't climb over, you can only fall.\nThe State is the borrower, the State is the King.\nThe little man doesn't get a single thing.\n[shouted] Not a single thing!\n[Guitar Solo]\n[Instrumental: Aggressive, blues-rock guitar solo. Dirty tone. Lots of feedback.]\n[Chorus]\n[Full Volume | Heavy Percussion]\nCrowded out!\nGot no room to breathe.\nCrowded out!\nGot no choice but to leave.\nThe Giant eats the grain, the mouse gets the husk\nThey closed the factory down at dusk.\nCrowded out!\n[Outro]\n[Rhythm: The beat gets slower and heavier]\n[Vocal: Ad-lib]\nShut it down.\nLock the gate.\nToo much debt.\nToo much state.\n[Sound: One final, loud SLAM of the metal shutter]\n[End]",
      "cleanLyrics": "[Intro]\n[Verse 1]\nThe Smith boys ran the shop for forty years\nThrough the blood and the sweat and the rust and the tears.\nThey weathered the storm and they weathered the drought\nBut the Big Man came and he crowded them out.\nI went to the bank with my hat in my hand\nNeed a little line just to work the land.\nThe banker said \"Son, I can't help you now\nThe Government bought the horse and the cow.\"\n[Chorus]\nCrowded out!\nGot no room to breathe.\nCrowded out!\nGot no choice but to leave.\nThe Giant eats the grain, the mouse gets the husk\nThey closed the factory down at dusk.\nYeah, the Treasury takes the silver and gold\nAnd leaves us out in the bitter cold.\nCrowded out!\n[Interlude]\n[Verse 2]\nOne Point Eight Trill-yun in the hole this year\nThat’s the deficit.\nSucking up the cash, making money dear.\nThe Credit Crunch is a heavy boot\nStomping on the neck of the local root.\nSubchapter Five filings on the rise\nThat's bankruptcy.\nWhile the stock market climbs to the empty skies.\nThey pave the road to a bridge nowhere\nWhile the shop floor sits in the silent air.\n[Bridge]\nThe interest rate is a nine percent wall.\nYou can't climb over, you can only fall.\nThe State is the borrower, the State is the King.\nThe little man doesn't get a single thing.\nNot a single thing!\n[Guitar Solo]\n[Chorus]\nCrowded out!\nGot no room to breathe.\nCrowded out!\nGot no choice but to leave.\nThe Giant eats the grain, the mouse gets the husk\nThey closed the factory down at dusk.\nCrowded out!\n[Outro]\nShut it down.\nLock the gate.\nToo much debt.\nToo much state.\n(Metal Shutter Slam)\n© 2025 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
      "musicalPrompt": "[Genre: Industrial Blues Rock]\n[Style: Stomp and Holler]\n[Mood: Angry, Gritty, Heavy, Resentful]\n[Vocalist: Male | Baritone | Raspy | Aggressive | Blue Collar]\n[Instrumentation: Distorted Harmonica, Metal Shutter Percussion, Heavy Boot Stomp, Slide Guitar, Industrial Noise]\n[Production: Raw, Lo-fi, Distorted, Heavy Bass]\n[Structure: Blues Structure, Call and Response]",
      "artPrompt": "A black-and-white photo of a weathered \"Going Out of Business\" sign taped to a dirty workshop window. Through the glass, we see idle, rusted machinery. Reflected in the glass is a shiny, modern government construction project across the street. High contrast, gritty texture. --ar 9:16\nI am glad the \"metal shutter\" percussion of Track 6 hit the mark. That aggressive stomp was necessary to close out Side B.\nNow, we flip the record to Side C: The Crash (The Minsky Moment) .\nWe are moving from the loud anger of the street to the quiet, sinister machinations of the \"back room.\""
