      "leadArchetype": "The Homesteader",
      "summary": "\"Ground Zero (The Foundation)\" is a song about the difference between price and value. In the wreckage of the crash, the protagonist realizes that while his stock portfolio (price) is zero, his ability to work, his community, and the land under his feet (value) remain. It is a rejection of \"Paper Promises\" in favor of \"Hard Truths.\" It sets the stage for the adoption of hard money (Gold/Bitcoin) in the final act.",
      "decoratedLyrics": "[Genre: Southern Rock, Soulful Blues]\n[Mood: Determined, Gritty, Rising, Anthemic]\n[Vocalist: Male | Baritone | Soulful | Strong]\n[Instrumentation: Acoustic Guitar intro building to Full Band, Hammond B3 Organ, Stomp Clap Rhythm, Slide Guitar]\n[Tempo: Medium | Building | 85 BPM]\n[Intro]\n[Instrumental: The lonely acoustic guitar from the last track plays a few bars. Then, a heavy kick drum beats once. Then again. Boom. Boom.]\n[Vocal: Hummed melody, low and resonant.]\n[Verse 1]\n[Vocal Delivery: Gritty | Resolve]\nThe paper burned up in the bonfire heat.\nBut the ground is still solid beneath my feet.\nThe zeros and ones that we saw on the screen\nWere just a ghost in the big machine.\nThey told us that wealth was a promise to pay\nBut promises break in the light of the day.\nI lost the illusion, I lost the facade\nBut I found the truth in the turning of the sod.\n[Chorus]\n[Instrumentation: Full band enters. Warm organ swells. Soulful backing vocals.]\n[Vocal Style: Belting | Passionate]\nWe’re back to Ground Zero, but the view is clear.\nNo more fog and no more fear.\nBuild it on rock, not on the sand\nBuild it with iron and the work of your hand.\nThe storm took the rot, and it washed it away\nWe are building the foundation of a brand new day.\nA brand new day.\n[Verse 2]\n[Vocal Delivery: Confident]\nYou can print a dollar, but you can't print a soul.\nYou can't print the gold that’s in the hole.\nYou can't print the sweat on a working man's brow\nAnd you can't print the time that is passing right now.\nWe traded the fake for the things that are real\nThe wood and the stone and the tempered steel.\nLet the gamblers cry for the chips they lost\nWe are counting the value, not the cost.\n[Bridge]\n[Tempo: Driving]\n[Vocal Style: Preaching]\nLet it fall! (Let it fall)\nLet it crash! (Let it crash)\nWe don't need the debt and we don't need the trash.\nFind the thing that cannot break.\nFind the thing they cannot take.\nImmutable.\nUndeniable.\nReal.\n[Guitar Solo]\n[Instrumental: A soaring, uplifting slide guitar solo. It sounds like sunrise.]\n[Chorus]\n[Maximum Volume | Choir backing]\nWe’re back to Ground Zero, but the view is clear.\nNo more fog and no more fear.\nBuild it on rock, not on the sand\nBuild it with iron and the work of your hand.\nThe storm took the rot, and it washed it away\nWe are building the foundation of a brand new day.\n[Outro]\n[Instrumentation: Instruments fade out one by one, leaving just the Kick Drum and the Organ.]\n[Vocal: Ad-lib fading]\nBack to the rock.\nBack to the real.\nGround Zero.\n[Sound: The sound of a hammer striking an anvil. Clang. Clang. Clang.]\n[End]",
